Yes. One possible loop is:

uncertainty → future-simulation → scanning for threat → difficulty trusting → inability to relax → more uncertainty.

The mind starts treating vigilance as the thing keeping you safe, so presence can feel almost irresponsible. Even when nothing is happening, part of you keeps asking, “What am I missing?” That is different from useful planning; planning has an endpoint. Hypervigilant thinking keeps reopening the case.

And trust is central. Relaxation requires some willingness to let reality proceed for a moment without supervising every branch. If you do not trust other people, the environment, or even your own ability to recover if something goes wrong, then the nervous system has a reason to keep watch.

The trap is that constant monitoring eventually produces its own evidence: when you are always scanning, you always find something ambiguous enough to justify another scan.

So the move is not necessarily “stop thinking about the future.” It is learning to distinguish preparedness from continuous rehearsal. You can remain capable without remaining activated.

A useful question in the moment is: “Is there an action available right now, or am I just running another simulation?” If there is an action, take it. If there is not, returning attention to what is actually present is not negligence; it is refusing to spend energy on a branch that does not currently exist.

And your “maybe you are, maybe you aren’t, who knows?” is important too. Sometimes the loop comes from trying to achieve impossible certainty about your own internal state. You do not always have to correctly diagnose yourself before you can notice: I’m looping right now. That observation alone is often enough to change the next move.